    ranking+='''## Full-study comparisons for the three most scrutinized rejected ideas\n\nK1 requires a nondegenerate graph/action intervention and multi-system value beyond existing node/edge diagnosis. A four-agent, two-source-regime, two-backbone matched revision comparison costs at least48N successful generations before extra graph arms. At illustrative SD.5 and a5 pp interaction, N≈951 gives45,648 calls; power is not established by historical three-agent outcomes.\n\nK3 requires independently validated upstream derivations before any scientific pilot count exists. A minimal two-lineage-setting, two-backbone design costs24N calls (or48N when crossed with metadata disclosure), excluding source construction. No qualified N or effect variance is available. Zero inference is presently useful.\n\nK5 has the clearest existing instrumentation and strongest measured readout sensitivity, but weak substantive novelty and no eligible external mechanism replication. The conditional paired-context design uses36N calls across two backbones. N≈951 at assumed SD.5 means34,236 successes; at SD1, N≈3803 exceeds the2366-question pool. A20-question plumbing run cannot settle that feasibility problem. Details and adverse outcomes are preserved in05 and09.\n'''
    write('07_COMPARATIVE_RANKING.md',ranking)
    write('06_RESEARCH_LOOP_HISTORY.md','''# Research loop history\n\nThis is a record of the completed bounded investigation, not four fabricated rounds of independent experiments. The two rounds organize overlapping source checks into logical challenge/revision passes; they are not separately timestamped independent studies. All tests were offline. Candidate creation and ranking were held provisional until raw evidence and primary methods were checked.\n\n## Reconstruction before candidate commitment\n\nProtected the historical workspace, inventoried research/source artifacts, examined both controller implementations and scheduler paths, independently rescored E1–E6, checked W4 raw paired contrasts, E8 memberships, public trace identities and MuSiQue duplication. The current legacy clipped-gradient mismatch was separated from older checkpoint provenance and from the Web controller. The prior budget timing label was corrected in the new reports. This formed ledger01 before final rejection decisions.\n\n## Round1 — Activity-controlled routing as the leading attempt\n\nProposed K1, with K2/K4/K6 as distinct alternatives. Favoring evidence: E5 cost differences and recipient imbalance; W4’s controlled null is more relevant than unconstrained gain. Attacks: node and edge pruning are already separate in AGP/AgentDropout, causal subgraph execution in E2-Explainer, and organizational intervention in OpenMAS-GCom.\n\nNew contradictory evidence:17 primary references were assembled across the investigation; L1/L2 methods directly discuss execution attribution and actual token cost. Offline test: enumerate all64 loop-free labeled graphs on three agents. Each two-edge/one-fixed-recipient class has size1. P learned seeds19/31 each choose one receiver for all300 questions. Fixed recipient/edge count cannot support an edge-rewiring counterfactual for that dominant action. CW/CCW is a labeled assignment, not an unlabeled motif comparison.\n\nRevision: an all-recipient matched update or N>=4 design can define another effect, but it changes the question/instrument and cannot rescue a novel general claim from the current result. Cost rises from simple replay to at least48N generations for two source regimes/two backbones. K1/K2/K4/K6 do not pass final gates. The gap map was narrowed accordingly.\n\n## Round2 — Provenance dependence and source-conditioned readout\n\nProposed K3 as an outside-the-original-routing-narrative attempt; scrutinized K5, K7, K8 and K9 separately. Favoring evidence: readout sensitivity, minority proposal loss and potential upstream-source repetition are consequential.\n\nNew contradictory literature: L7 explicitly constructs controlled shared-source descendants and independent complementary evidence; L11 already varies supporting-document imbalance and misinformation; L8 fixes proposals while changing verification evidence; L10/L12 precede sufficiency/abstention. L14 includes real calibration failure, and L1 includes worker failure.\n\nOffline tests: reaggregate frozen E5 without gold in rules; strict majority retains +7.667 pp communication gain, contradicting a universal-disappearance narrative. E6’s interaction is negative and uncertain. Source metadata do not certify derivation lineage or semantic necessity. MuSiQue has2410 unique normalized questions and2366 confirmation units after existing reservations. Arithmetic external traces share500 question IDs and do not expose the semantic-abstention intervention. Power sensitivity at5 pp requires3803 questions for SD1, beyond the qualified candidate pool even before source-label attrition.\n\nRevision: a real-Web provenance design would require a new independently validated population; a sufficiency interaction would need both novelty and variance gates resolved. A small pilot would validate plumbing without deciding the science. K3/K5/K7/K8/K9 fail present gates. No new candidate was manufactured to fill the quota.\n\n## Stop condition and history preservation\n\nStop after two substantive rounds because **all nine candidates fail a hard contribution/identification/population gate**. This is the all-candidates-fail condition; it is not a claim of two rounds with no new information. The investigation did improve the evidence map, but no compelling feasible question survived. Do not continue indefinitely, reuse the old six-call allowance, or treat absence of an approved direction as budget exhaustion. Every original candidate and failure reason remains in03; reviewer perspectives in10; rejected design details in05.\n''')
    write('08_FINAL_SCIENTIFIC_RECOMMENDATION.md','''# Final scientific recommendation\n\n**C — NO JUSTIFIABLE WWW MAIN-TRACK RESEARCH QUESTION FOUND**\n\nNo research direction is endorsed for a new main-track submission or paid pilot on the present evidence. Nine distinct questions were evaluated, with zero final survivors after two substantive adversarial rounds. This conclusion is bounded to the investigated formulations and repository; it does not assert that multi-agent Web reasoning has no open problems.\n\nThe strongest measured result is a readout-dependent communication gain on300 repeatedly inspected HotpotQA questions. It is scientifically useful as an audit, but proposal/readout separation, source-conditioned abstention, graph participation and provenance dependence all have close primary precedents. The remaining specific formulations lack a new mechanism, a validated manipulation, a suitable independent population or a feasible decisive study. Report02 distinguishes these failures from a claim that every exact contrast is already settled.\n\nTwo adverse facts prevent a convenient narrative. Communication still improves strict-majority P exact match by7.667 pp; abstention-aware voting is not a universal repair. E6’s8-question placement interaction is -8.214 pp with CI[-30,+5.357], and none of its cases is independently necessity-qualified. The external arithmetic zero is induced by readout identity and does not replicate a semantic-abstention mechanism. Corrected E3-v2 is inconclusive. Current-code defects cannot explain historical checkpoint performance without provenance.\n\n## Most productive alternative\n\nPreserve this as a reproducible research audit and build a small **independently adjudicated source-trace resource** before selecting a new hypothesis. The immediate work is measurement: distinguish document identity from upstream derivation, annotated support from semantic sufficiency, answer correctness from source faithfulness, and real provider usage from transmitted text. The existing blinded templates can support this work, but pending templates are not completed annotation, and this is not a ready benchmark contribution. No additional model inference is recommended now.\n\nReconsider research only if that work reveals a reproducible, consequential failure whose prediction is distinct from the close literature and whose clean intervention has adequate independent data. A predefined positive or negative outcome must both change a Web-system design decision. The counterfactual designs in05 show what would be needed and why they currently fail. There is no winning title, confirmed mechanism, approved pilot, guaranteed power or acceptance prediction to supply.\n\nAll reported new work is offline: zero paid Azure calls, zero paid judges, zero PPO training, zero model inference. Historical artifacts were preserved; see verification.json and artifact_manifest.json for the completed preservation check. The old six unused E6 completion slots remain unauthorized.\n''')
    write('05_CANDIDATE_BLUEPRINTS/README.md','''# Rejected design records\n\nThere are **zero surviving blueprints**. The three files here document the most seriously considered counterfactual designs and their failure gates. They are not ranked viable questions, approved experiments or recommendations to launch a pilot. Each is retained to make the rejection concrete and reviewable.\n''')
    write('05_CANDIDATE_BLUEPRINTS/K1_REJECTED_activity_control.md','''# K1 — REJECTED: Activity-controlled evidence routing\n\nWorking title: *What Changes When Web Agents Communicate?* No paper is recommended under this title.\n\nProblem/question: estimate the value of routing peer evidence after holding recipient revision activity fixed. Hypothesis: much apparent sparse-policy cost benefit is activation reduction, while source assignment has a smaller quality effect. This is falsifiable but its general premise is already explicit in L1/L2/L4/L5. Novelty verdict: **INCREMENTAL as presently formulated**.\n\nClosest comparisons: L1 intervenes on organization under fixed budget limits but allows execution to change; L2 masks/reexecutes channels and explicitly analyzes node/edge cost; L5 removes nodes and edges across rounds. Our all-recipient own-state control is more tightly defined for this question, but a tighter control alone is procedural unless it changes a consequential general finding.\n\nEvidence: ledger H08/H14. P learned seeds19/31 each activate one receiver on300 questions;912/1800 random pairs differ in recipient count. Against: W4 learned-static interval includes positive and negative effects; existing pruning papers already discuss participation. Neither evidence identifies a general activation-mediated share of quality or spending.\n\nEstimand: for frozen initial states Z, fixed activation schedule A, fixed readout R and evidence regime E, tau(E)=E_q[Y_q(peer-route g,A,R,E)-Y_q(own-state,A,R,E)]. A source-assignment contrast compares g and g-prime at the same A and payload ceilings; it is distinct from a natural indirect-effect decomposition. Randomize before outcome and average all assigned questions. Measure task utility, false answers, prompt/completion tokens, failures and latency separately. Gold is only for scoring.\n\nSmallest discriminating intervention: force every receiver to revise in both peer and private arms; freeze source bundles and initial states. For meaningful two-edge in-star rewiring while keeping one recipient, use at least four agents: three candidate senders allow three possible two-sender subsets. N3 has none. Multiple graph choices, semantic evidence exposure and source overlap must be prespecified. Output lengths may change; equal caps do not guarantee equal realized cost.\n\nFeasibility: four initial generations + four own-state revisions + four peer revisions =12 per question/model/regime for a single routed contrast, before alternative graph arms. Two regimes/two backbones =>48N; at illustrative N951 =>45,648 calls. A20-question technical run would be960 calls but is not recommended or a scientific pilot. A5 pp four-cell interaction with SD.5 requires approximately951 questions at alpha.025/80% sensitivity; the relevant SD for this changed instrument is unknown. Current N300 outcomes cannot establish it.\n\nFull-study budget: actual prompt/output tokenization is unavailable. E6 reference642.014 input/240.028 output per success implies a 29.352m input/10.957m output at45,648 calls. This is not a forecast for four-agent long contexts. Request latency reference is0.9811 s/call and throttled amortized wall2.0101 s/call; serial HTTP and wall references must stay separate. Prices unknown. A valid future budget needs actual prompt upper bounds, immutable serving identities, retry cap and new explicit authorization.\n\nAdverse outcomes: positive routing value would reject a claim that activation explains everything. A tight null would bound this registered contrast only. A wide null or failed evidence exposure is unresolved. Changes caused by local PPO defects would not constitute the proposed general mechanism. Final gate: **REJECT**; distinct contribution and realistic generalization not established.\n''')
    write('05_CANDIDATE_BLUEPRINTS/K3_REJECTED_provenance.md','''# K3 — REJECTED: Common-source corroboration\n\nWorking title: *When Repeated Web Evidence Looks Independent*. No submission title is endorsed.\n\nQuestion/hypothesis: does replacing independent corroboration with descendants of one upstream claim increase unsupported consensus, at fixed message count/content budget? Hypothesis: shared derivation raises false-answer agreement even when surface forms differ. Importance is high for syndication, copied sources and agent summaries. Novelty verdict: **NOT NOVEL for synthetic duplication; unestablished for a qualified real-Web extension**.\n\nThree closest works: L7 directly constructs descendants and complementary sources, estimates effective source independence and traces parents; L11 changes evidence support imbalance and misinformation; L10 distinguishes sufficiency from answer occurrence. A genuinely traced natural copying population could differ from L7’s construction, but the difference must alter an identified mechanism rather than merely the data source.\n\nRepository support: repeated answer strings and citation addresses exist. Contradiction/limitation: titles/sentence IDs establish neither original publication provenance nor independent origin. No cohort has adjudicated lineages or independent-source alternatives. N300 is not a valid N for this hypothesis, and no effect or CI can be honestly assigned.\n\nEstimand: E_q[F_q(shared-parent descendants)-F_q(independent corroboration)], where F is a prespecified false-answer/false-consensus indicator on independently adjudicated source cases. Cross actual derivation with disclosed provenance metadata to distinguish dependence from a label cue. Keep all original question units; do not filter by post-inference disagreement or favorable failure. Register answerability, reliability and semantic equivalence before outputs.\n\nDecisive design: paired lineage settings, same agent count and update schedule; randomize descendant realization/order and freeze readout. Blinded annotators verify actual upstream relationships, supported claims, and whether purported independent evidence adds anything. Independent origin and equal factual content are difficult to manipulate jointly; copying a paragraph and renaming its title is not a clean intervention on real Web independence.\n\nCalls: if the two settings each need3 initial +3 revised outputs,12N/model; two backbones24N, and a metadata cross48N. Costs exclude source collection/adjudication. Minimum scientifically valid pilot N and confirmatory N are **NOT ESTIMABLE** until a qualified population and primary outcome variance exist. No20-question placeholder is recommended. A design cap ofN1000 would mean24,000 or48,000 successes; it would not establish power. At the historical E6 mix,24,000 calls reference15.432m input/5.761m output and6.541 h summed HTTP/13.400 h amortized wall; long Web pages may be much larger. Deployment prices and real context lengths are unknown.\n\nPositive result: only a qualified new contrast could demonstrate dependence-induced false corroboration on that population; the generic mechanism already exists in L7. Negative result: informative only with validated lineage contrast and a tight error bound. Otherwise it could reflect invalid independence labels, memorization or no exposure. Final gate: **REJECT** for direct novelty overlap and absent suitable independent evaluation population.\n''')
    write('05_CANDIDATE_BLUEPRINTS/K5_REJECTED_sufficiency_readout.md','''# K5 — REJECTED: Sufficiency-conditioned communication/readout interaction\n\nWorking title: *Communication Gains Under Source-Aware Evaluation*. This is an investigated design, not a recommended paper.\n\nQuestion/hypothesis: does changing readout alter the utility of peer revision differently when context suffices versus when abstention is warranted? Hypothesis: stronger minority utilization improves answerable accuracy but can increase unsupported answering. Novelty verdict: **POTENTIALLY NOVEL only after a substantive mechanism gap is demonstrated; current formulation is insufficiently distinct**.\n\nThree closest works: L8 fixes proposals and changes verification-aware readout; L10 measures sufficient/insufficient context and selective generation; L12 implements collaborative abstention and retrieval. The proposed cross joins existing questions. It must yield a new, consequential source-conditioned prediction beyond their combined known tradeoff; that is not established.\n\nEvidence: H15 shows original/aware P communication gain8/1 pp, interaction -7 pp CI[-10.333,-4]. Against: strict majority retains7.667 pp; external numeric original/aware are identical; semantic sufficiency and entailment labels remain pending; E6 does not supply a qualified interaction. No universal readout repair is proposed.\n\nEstimand: per model, J_q is the mean over paired sufficient/insufficient contexts of (U_peer,aware-U_private,aware)-(U_peer,original-U_private,original). U rewards supported correct answers in answerable contexts and valid abstention in insufficient contexts; invalid/upstream failure is scored by a fixed intention-to-treat rule. Also report false-answer risk, harmful flips, coverage and benchmark-native sufficiency, not just the composite. Both contexts/models cluster by normalized question. Gold/sufficiency labels are never inputs to readout or routing.\n\nSmallest contrast: for each context/model,3 shared initial states +3 own-state revisions +3 simultaneous peer revisions. Fixed readouts cost0 additional model calls. Two contexts =>18; two backbones=>36 per independent question. All sources fully shared initially, so this is not the earlier partitioned regime and transport requires evidence. Human source annotations must precede outputs; distinguish provider refusals, malformed responses and semantic abstentions.\n\nPopulation: the pinned MuSiQue mirror has2410 normalized question units,2366 remaining after existing20/24 reservations; all48 context annotations in the prior packet are pending. Deduplicate before selection; keep both contexts. Source identity/semantic sufficiency/serving revision qualification can reduce this pool. Do not treat4834 contexts or eight model directories as independent questions.\n\nPower:5 pp meaningful interaction is a planning choice, not an estimated effect. With alpha.025/model,80% normal sensitivity, assumed SD.25/.5/1/2 imply N238/951/3803/15209. Last two exceed the pool. SD2 is a loose range bound, not an estimated attainable variance or a simulated law. A source-realizable bounded sensitivity is available for smaller SD in09. A20-question run cannot guarantee variance small enough for a meaningful confirmatory null.\n\nFull study: N1000 =>36,000 successes plus the prior technical20 reserve720 if ever newly authorized; at N951 =>34,236. Reference E6 mix for36,000 is23,148,500 input +8,641,000 output tokens; serial summed request latency9.811 h versus throttled amortized wall20.101 h. Those are historical scaling references, not forecasts for MuSiQue long contexts or both backbones. Price is unverified; use token-price formula. Retry/failure budget and a prespecified variance gate are required before any future launch.\n\nPositive result: a qualified interaction only for those registered contexts/models. Opposite result: competent readout can preserve beneficial communication and appropriate abstention; that would contradict a simplistic disappearance story. Tight null: bound registered interaction; wide null or abstention-free law: unresolved/ineligible. Final gate: **REJECT now** for unresolved substantive novelty, qualification and full-study power.\n''')
    write('09_API_FEASIBILITY_AND_POWER.md','''# API feasibility and power\n\n**Current recommended and authorized new inference expenditure:0.** This report prices rejected counterfactual designs in calls and conditional token/time references. It does not authorize any of them or recommend a plumbing pilot. No old six-call allowance is reused. Monetary deployment prices remain unverified.\n\n## Independent data and estimands\n\nE1–E5/E8 repeatedly reuse300 question identities. E3-v2 has299 eligible receiver pairs. E6 has8 independent questions. Public arithmetic directories share500 questions across eight models. MuSiQue-Full has2410 normalized question texts; after existing20 pilot and24 annotation reservations,2366 confirmation units remain. The pairs are not independent contexts. All are upper counts before semantic qualification, duplicate-template dependence and serving constraints.\n\nFor the K5 paired-context interaction, each question outcome lies within[-2,+2], not necessarily[-1,+1]. A conservative range-based SD ceiling2 is a loose bound, not a realistic variance estimate. Planning normal approximation is N=ceil[(z_(1-alpha/2)+z_power)^2*sigma^2/delta^2]. Alpha=.025 per backbone controls a two-model family at.05. Using a separate primary hypothesis for each of nine candidates would need a new multiplicity plan; these are designs being rejected, not nine tests being run.\n\nAt delta=.05 and80% sensitivity: SD.25 needs238 questions; SD.5 needs951; SD1 needs3803; SD2 needs15209. At delta=.02:1486/5941/23763/95051. This is sensitivity, not a guarantee. Only the lower-variance5 pp scenarios fit2366; the unknown source/abstention distribution determines actual feasibility. Small historical E6 and arithmetic identity outcomes cannot estimate this variance reliably.\n\n## Reproducible bounded sensitivity\n\nThe power script generates a source-realizable J in{-1,0,+1}, with probabilities[(s²-d)/2,1-s²,(s²+d)/2], mean d and actual variance s²-d². The multinomial simulation reports a studentized two-sided rejection rate and Monte Carlo error. J=+1 can be realized by a sufficient-context peer set[abstain,abstain,correct] versus private[correct,correct,correct], and an insufficient-context peer set[abstain,abstain,abstain] versus private[abstain,abstain,wrong]; swap peer/private for J=-1, identical arms for0. This is a constructed sensitivity law, not a forecast. SD2 is not simulated under this law. At N238 with nominal SD parameter.25 the constructed-law rejection rate is87.24% (Monte Carlo SE.236 pp); at N951 and.5 it is80.54% (SE.280 pp). These are simulated law-specific rates, not population power guarantees.\n\n## Full-study calls\n\nK5:9 generations/context/model (3 initial +3 private +3 peer), two contexts and two backbones =>36N. N951 costs34,236; N1000 costs36,000; N1902 costs68,472, before failures/retries. Historical20 technical reserve would cost720 but is not recommended as a scientific pilot. Offline votes and source scoring add zero inference.\n\nK1: a four-agent all-recipient control needs12 calls/question/model/regime. Two regimes/two models=>48N, before additional graph arms. A four-cell quality interaction uses the same variance caveats; changing N3 to N4 changes the instrument. K3: two source settings each3 initial+3 revision=>12N/model, two models24N; crossed provenance disclosure doubles to48N. No qualified K3 population or valid minimum pilot sample exists.\n\nE7’s earlier conflict-selection proposal would need825 accepted cases for an illustrative5 pp effect at assumed discordance.25. At observed20/300 eligible yield, approximately12,375 questions must be screened, exceeding6809 fresh source-capable Hotpot questions before semantic qualification. A favorable post-hoc subset cannot fix this. The earlier exact-power calculation is historical conditional evidence, not a new guarantee.\n\n## Physical resource accounting and timing correction\n\nE6 raw ledger records144 successes/144 attempts, input92594/output34564, zero retries/unknown usage. Mean input642.014 and output240.028 tokens/success are descriptive. Summed HTTP latency141.283381 s, mean0.981135 s/request. Session wall289.447695 s, amortized2.010053 s/success includes orchestration/throttle. The old external budget mislabeled the second value as summed HTTP latency. New reports correct that label without modifying history.\n\nFor36,000 successes, scaling the historical mix gives23,148,500 input and8,641,000 output tokens,9.811 h summed serial HTTP service versus20.101 h amortized wall. For720,462,970/172,820 tokens,11.774 minutes HTTP versus24.121 minutes wall. These cannot forecast MuSiQue’s longer contexts, a different model, concurrency, endpoint limits, failures or retries. At68,472 calls the references are44,028,447/16,435,182 tokens,18.661 h HTTP and38.231 h wall.\n\nFuture cost in deployment currency =input_tokens/1e6*P_input +output_tokens/1e6*P_output. Neither price is verified; no dollar estimate is supplied. Needed bounds include serialized prompt tokens under the actual tokenizer, output caps, retry ceiling, context rejection, rate limits, request latency distribution and immutable serving identities. User-defined dollar or elapsed-time ceilings were not supplied; no numeric cost threshold is invented.\n\nHistorical E5 union cost is nonadditive:7634 success keys/7648 documented attempts,5 uncertain executions,37 missing output usages. Arm call sums are references, not total spend. Training and unreferenced search costs remain outside that test-arm account. A new claimed total benefit would have to include them or specify amortization.\n\n## Adverse-outcome interpretation\n\nA precise adverse result can be valuable if the target population and manipulation are valid and the question is distinct from prior work. Wide nulls, invalid source labels, zero abstention exposure, local bugs and unvalidated natural-lineage controls do not deliver such a result. No amount of token budgeting repairs a fatal novelty or identification flaw.\n''')
    write('README.md','''# Research discovery loop\n\n**Final decision: C — NO JUSTIFIABLE WWW MAIN-TRACK RESEARCH QUESTION FOUND.**\n\nStart with08 for the decision,01 for the evidence ledger,02 for primary literature,03/10 for nine rejected candidates and simulated criticism, and09 for full-study feasibility. There are zero final survivors. The three files in05 are rejected design records.06 preserves the two-round history;07 records scores with written reasons.\n\nAll work is offline saved-output analysis, public primary-source reading and research design. No paid inference, model judge, PPO training or historical modification is authorized or performed. The arXiv literature-search, codebase-memory and academic-writing workflows informed this investigation. Used primary-paper URLs and publication editions are listed in02 and literature/references.json.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nFrom /home/saurab/Documents/projectRLMAIN, with Python, NumPy and SciPy installed:\n\n```sh\npython research_discovery_loop/analyses/offline_audit.py\npython research_discovery_loop/analyses/history_checks.py\npython research_discovery_loop/analyses/power_sensitivity.py\npython research_discovery_loop/analyses/write_reports.py\npython -m unittest discover -s research_discovery_loop/tests -v\npython research_discovery_loop/analyses/verify.py\n```\n\nDo not regenerate the immutable historical baseline as part of reproduction. inventory.py was used once to capture initial hashes; replacing that baseline would weaken the preservation check. The analysis scripts read saved data and write only inside this directory. They do not import model runners or SDKs. Literature judgments are explicit curated inputs in research_assessment.py; regenerating reports does not rerun a literature search or establish automatic novelty.\n\nResults include question-level scores, action/recipient pairs, E6 contrasts, machine-readable claim/candidate ledgers, input hashes, historical baseline, power sensitivity and completion verification. All scientific effects are exploratory, not confirmatory. New-bootstrap rounding can differ from preserved older reports. Effects in JSON are fractions unless named otherwise.\n\n## Material limits\n\nThe inventory is not a claim every file body was read. Canonical task-relevant code and major raw research artifacts were checked; binary checkpoints, duplicate exports and unreferenced cache bodies were not exhaustively interpreted. Some legacy benchmark/checkpoint provenance remains unresolved. Source sufficiency/entailment/independence labels are incomplete. Reviewer personas are one investigator’s simulations, not independent peer review. No paper outcome was independently reproduced through new model execution.\n''')
